# 💭 Reflection

Today I built and tested a Windows file-sharing environment using SERVER01 and WIN11-CLIENT01.

I learned how a Windows shared folder is accessed through SMB and how a UNC path such as `\\SERVER01\Finance` provides direct access to a shared resource.

One useful troubleshooting experience was discovering that the Finance share could be opened directly using the Run dialog even though SERVER01 was not visible through the File Explorer Network view. This demonstrated that Network Discovery and direct SMB share access are separate concepts.

I also reinforced the relationship between Active Directory security groups and resource permissions. Instead of assigning access directly to individual users, the lab uses the `GG-Finance` security group to control access to the Finance resource.

The main lesson from today's lab was to use evidence-driven troubleshooting. A missing computer from the Network view does not automatically mean that the file server or SMB share is unavailable. Testing the actual UNC path provided evidence that the share itself was accessible.

This lab connected several previously learned concepts—Active Directory, security groups, Windows networking, SMB, permissions, authentication, authorization, and Help Desk troubleshooting—into one practical scenario.

